diff --git a/CHANGELOG.md b/CHANGELOG.md
index 78316b2a..f2a6e582 100644
--- a/CHANGELOG.md
+++ b/CHANGELOG.md
@@ -5,6 +5,7 @@ Each release's page on GitHub has its notes and measurements. See [the 1.0.0 rel
 ## Unreleased
 
 - speech: a voice file says whose voice it holds. `source` is `{kind: model-invented | owner | consented, note}`, the operator's attestation, and `--voices` refuses a file without one or with a blank note. `GET /v1/voices` lists the loaded voices (name, frames, seconds, source kind); there is no upload route. `tools/zig/drama_voice_clip.py` prepares a take: mono 44.1 kHz aimed at -20 dBFS speech level within -1 dBFS peaks, cut at a pause to a 10-30 s clip, refused when short, clipped, noisy (judged on the kept clip), over music or hum, or voiced by more than one speaker. Its transcript is either the take's, kept only when the cut leaves no loud frame out, or a local Whisper draft to correct; a confirmed transcript is never paired with a re-prepared clip. `drama_voice.py` encodes with `--reference` or `--native` when given, else with a `tensorfold-native` on `PATH` whose `--help` lists `voice encode` ([docs](docs/drama-voices.md))
+- speech: `tensorfold-native voice encode CLIP.wav --model DIR --text T --name NAME --out NAME.json` writes a Drama 3 voice file with a native CUDA codec encoder (DAC encoder, pre_module and RVQ in BF16), no Python; 2.5 to 3 % of its semantic codes differ from the reference's ([docs](docs/drama-voices.md))
 - speech: the Drama 3 backbone's full attention runs the reference's softmax schedule: one running max per 64-key tile, with the reference kernel's exp, division, gate sigmoid and fused multiply-adds. On our GB10, the attention operator now matches the reference on 98.1% and 96.2% of its outputs at layers 3 and 39 (was 70.4% and 57.5%), and on our 182-token text fixture the backbone's distance to the reference fell from 0.124 to 0.053 relative L2 (semantic KL 0.024 to 0.0062, worst row cosine 0.80 to 0.92). Rows keep their bits whatever the chunk size, and decode uses the same arithmetic.
 - speech: a streamed Drama 3 reply that collapses into silence now ends at the same frame as the whole reply of the same generation: the frame that completes 10 s of silence. Before, on our GB10 with a test reply, the stream ended 12.7 s later, because the silence guard read each chunk after the stream's client had begun converting it to PCM16 in place. The guard now hears each chunk before it is handed over ([docs](docs/drama-voices.md#silence-guard))
 - speech: a Drama 3 synthesis that stays silent (below -60 dBFS) for 10 s ends there with `X-Finish-Reason: silence` and keeps its audio. Before, a collapsed reply generated up to a minute of near-digital silence before its end of speech or the frame limit. A stall that recovers sooner (one measured stall lasted 7.4 s) is kept untouched. Silence reached in the last frames still counts, ahead of the end of speech and the frame limit. With `--long-form`, such a segment ends the reply. To hear the audio while generating, a whole reply now decodes every 16 frames, with the incremental decode streams already use. On the 48-request default-voice capture, all 46 normal replies were byte-identical to the previous build, and the reply that stayed silent for 37 s was cut 10 s into its stall, a byte-identical prefix ([docs](docs/drama-voices.md#silence-guard))
diff --git a/docs/drama-voices.md b/docs/drama-voices.md
index e2d2f55f..a46cdca1 100644
--- a/docs/drama-voices.md
+++ b/docs/drama-voices.md
@@ -1,7 +1,7 @@
 # Drama 3 voices
 
 Drama 3 can speak in a reference voice. The prompt carries a clip's codec codes and the clip's transcript, the way the pinned reference (sglang-omni `prompt.py`, `build_prompt_rows`) builds it.
-The native server does not encode audio. A clip is prepared and encoded once, offline, into a voice file.
+The server does not encode audio during a request. A clip is prepared and encoded once, offline, into a voice file.
 
 ## Whose voice
 
@@ -53,6 +53,16 @@ python3 tools/zig/drama_voice.py --checkpoint DRAMA3_DIR --audio clips/NAME.wav
 - `--reference` runs the reference server's own encode for a `references[].audio_path`: `load_audio` at the codec's 44.1 kHz in mono, then `FishReferenceEncodeHook` under bfloat16 autocast on CUDA (the reference's default `reference_encode_dtype`). With `--device cpu` it encodes in float32. A 15.7 s clip takes about 2.6 GB of device memory and 10 to 20 s, most of it loading `codec.pth`.
 - Clips longer than 60 s are refused, as the reference refuses them. Leading and trailing white space in the transcript is dropped.
 
+Without Python, the native binary encodes a WAV clip (PCM or IEEE float, any rate, channels averaged) on CUDA:
+
+```sh
+tensorfold-native voice encode clip.wav --model DRAMA3_DIR --text "the clip's exact words" --name NAME --out voices/NAME.json
+```
+
+It runs the same encode in BF16 with the reference's roundings, in under 1 GB of device memory and about 4.5 s for a 50 s clip.
+Its codes are not yet identical to the reference's: on three clips 2.5 to 3 % of semantic codes and 3 to 14 % of residual codes differ.
+Run alone it writes no `source`, so `--voices` refuses the file; `drama_voice.py --native BINARY` runs it and adds one.
+
 A voice file is JSON with these fields:
 
 - `format`: `drama3-voice/1`.
